  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "is BAMS a good career option"</h2>

  <p>
    BAMS is a genuinely strong career choice in India, but the honest answer depends heavily on why someone is
    choosing it. Chosen deliberately, because Ayurvedic medicine genuinely interests you, it offers real career
    breadth and income potential. Chosen purely as a fallback for a missed MBBS seat, it tends to lead to a weaker,
    less satisfying career.
  </p>

  <p>
    The most important reframe: BAMS is not a lesser version of MBBS. It is a distinct medical system with its own
    scope of practice, own postgraduate route, and own growing sectors — wellness tourism, Ayurvedic product
    consulting, panchakarma specialisation — that MBBS graduates do not have direct access to.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This is not the "BAMS is just as good as MBBS" reassurance some college marketing leans on, and it is not the
      dismissive "BAMS is only for those who couldn't get MBBS" framing either. Both miss the real picture.
    </p>
    <p>
      BAMS genuinely leads to a different career with a different, often lower, average pay ceiling than MBBS in
      urban corporate-hospital settings — and it is also a genuinely respected, viable medical career on its own
      terms for someone who chose it deliberately. Both things are true at once.
    </p>
  </div>

