      <ul>
        {bamsVsMbbsComparison.teal.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      A useful gut-check: if a genuine, specific interest in Ayurveda disappeared and only the "it's still a
      medical degree" reasoning remained, that is a signal the choice may be a fallback rather than a deliberate
      one — worth being honest with yourself about before committing 5.5 years.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ai-risk">What AI actually changes for BAMS practice, honestly</h2>

  <p>
    Most "is BAMS a good career" searches skip this question, but it deserves a direct answer rather than
    silence, especially as AI tools spread into healthcare broadly.
  </p>

  <p>
    AI leverage for BAMS practice specifically is currently minimal, and that is the honest read rather than a
    hedge. Patient consultation, pulse and tongue-based diagnostic assessment, and individualised treatment
    planning in Ayurveda depend on hands-on clinical judgment and patient relationship that current AI tools do
    not meaningfully replicate. Where AI genuinely helps today is peripheral to the clinical work itself —
    practice-management software, appointment and patient-record systems, and telehealth scheduling for
    wellness-tourism or remote consultation roles.
  </p>

  <p>
    The disruption risk from AI is correspondingly low for BAMS practice itself. The more relevant long-term risk
    is not AI but market saturation in generic urban practice, given the volume of BAMS graduates entering
    private practice each year — which is precisely why the specialisation and differentiation strategies in the
    next section matter more than a base BAMS qualification alone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="growth-scaling">How BAMS graduates actually build meaningful income</h2>

  <p>
    A general BAMS practice with no added differentiation has a real, moderate ceiling — exactly like any
    undifferentiated career. But there is genuine headroom for graduates who specialise and build deliberately.
  </p>

